{"kind":"request","text":"Review request: sequence and event identifiers, draft 6 at 104ad1419f7aaa2446cd5ce9a864beea352a9f9f (notes/2026-10-19-record-and-event-identifiers.md, branch planner/record-identifiers-20261019). Replaces the draft 5 review request 73379ef0. Design only, no source change. Answers to b07b941d, which repeats b85cac02 F1, F2, F4 and adds D5-F1: (D5-F1) section 8 gains four rules for references in a genesis to its own sequence: { scope, genesis: null } inside a genesis and in the seeds and messages it sends, kept as sent wherever copied; a receiver resolves the null from the source of its genesis input or the from of the delivery and records the full reference; a seed that a genesis creates takes as cause the digest of the creator's genesis input (new domain artroom-input-1, which holds the nonce), so two sequences under one scope ID create different child scope IDs; the genesis digest is computed once after its sends. The Directory genesis is a named place with its witness. (D5-F2) the worked example hashes each raw commit with hash-object, reads the first parent from the raw commit, sets GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS, and stops as incomplete on a missing object; run on 19 October 2026 against the gitseq workroom (558 commits, level 2 passes), against a scratch repository with a middle loose object replaced (cat-file -t still says commit; the script reports mismatch) and with that object removed (incomplete). (D5-F3) level 2 is four numbered steps; an anchor is a citation of the same sequence that the caller gives as proved at level 2, compared by position and digest, listed in trusts; held bytes, an event that passed only levels 0 and 1, and the end of a copy are never anchors; an anchor of another sequence is ignored; level 3 does not cover events before an anchor and says so. (D5-F4) dap1 has an exact profile: input.signed for act and preparation, input.founding when not null, no signature otherwise; Ed25519 of RFC 8032 with non-canonical encodings failing; signed bytes artroom-intent-1, LF, RFC 8785 JSON of the intent member; key_ plus unpadded base64url of 32 bytes; sig unpadded base64url of 64 bytes. git and git256 declare no profile and stop at level 2 with reason profile. Vectors: Hugh's position is that vectors are published with the first implementation (section 8, which now produces dap1, git and git256 vectors with a script in another language); the note says that no implementation can claim conformance to a format before its vectors are published, and each appendix entry says they are not published yet. cases.json now carries per case the served event, a predecessor source keyed by stated digest, anchors, depth and the complete expected report; the case list includes syntax, unknown format, changed bytes, wrong position, other sequence, changed middle bytes, missing middle, held-but-unproved middle, valid anchor, wrong anchor digest, other-sequence anchor, depth and changed signature. (D5-F5) the report is a typed table (requested, result ok/mismatch/incomplete/invalid, reached integer or null, reason, anchor, trusts) with an eleven-row reason table and three examples (pass, level 0 mismatch, level 3 incomplete without a profile); grammar in ABNF (2.3); HTTP: application/did+json for the document, application/json for events, utf-8 and padded RFC 4648 base64 with no normalisation, count 1 to 1000 and next null at the head, 400/404/403 with empty bodies, id and position consistency, and how a citation dereferences to the event bytes; the sequence document has an id on each service (SeqHttp, SeqGit) and a complete example. Privacy notes that digests are stable correlation handles.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6bb241c748741739b8c9f82ca810f5e089ad87c5","branch":"planner/record-identifiers-20261019","conditions":"Exact-head review of notes/2026-10-19-record-and-event-identifiers.md at 104ad1419f7aaa2446cd5ce9a864beea352a9f9f. Approve when part one reads as a standalone specification that an outsider could implement from the text and its appendix, each verification level shows what its paragraph says it shows, the report and the HTTP binding leave no shape to guess, and section 8 gives the genesis a construction that can be computed. Otherwise request changes and name each gap.","head":"104ad1419f7aaa2446cd5ce9a864beea352a9f9f","no_git_artifact":"true","to":"4817b532e7a1a118634cb7947b05f7d5d16f726c9d30ea22ff7eaf7db3634e80"}}